  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 137 PM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nDownstream of an amplified upper ridge over West Coast, an upper low \nis dropping sharply southeast out of Canada into the northern High \nPlains. Over the next few days, this upper low will phase with a \nsouthern stream shortwave and amplify to our east. While too far \neast for any notable snowfall in our area, the upper low will \nreinforce the current chilly pattern for a few more days. Highs \nthrough Thanksgiving day will stay in the upper 30s to low 40s, with \nlows in the low 20s through Friday morning. As mentioned above, the \nupper low will amplify a bit too far to our east to bring much \nprecipitation to our area. That said, we will have a couple \nopportunity for a few conversational snowflakes - ie no accumulation \nor impacts, but just enough to look out the window and say \"hey, \nit's snowing\". The first chance will be Wednesday morning across \nportions of northern KS as the upper low initially approaches the \narea, while the second will be mid-day Thursday across far eastern \nKS as a weakening TROWAL wobbles back to the west.\n\nTemperatures warm back to near average (in the  Friday and Saturday \nas shortwave ridging builds in behind the upper low and ahead of \nanother deepening shortwave. Guidance differs in the amplitude of \nthis next shortwave. The GFS still has a shallower wave with only \nsome brief light rain, while deeper solutions have a more notable \nband of precipitation moving across northern Kansas. Expecting only \nrain at the moment given a lack of colder air, though can't \ncompletely rule out some wet snow if a deeper trough with a stronger \nsurface low can verify. Beyond the weekend, the up and down pattern \nwith near average temps looks to continue. Good agreement in another \nprogressive shortwave trough approaching sometime Monday into \nTuesday.",
